#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079420} :activity/id #uuid "5f643ade-7715-5788-91bb-d00dfcf9badd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943 :topic/id #uuid "cdbe026a-f2a4-5c0a-8b9f-a7d7b8060500" :topic/math-academy-id 625} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078276 :question/id #uuid "82814727-3ad9-5e62-a491-086484491d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19695af-0879-5a00-b400-1c4a490f7bfa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512867}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"} :step/id #uuid "e52ecac1-55d5-5dc3-a7ce-c1a112c5ca79" :step/math-academy-id 11988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079615 :tutorial/content "In general, the limit of a function $f(x)$ at a point only exists if *both* the left-sided and right-sided limits *exist* and are the *same*.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)$ for the function whose graph is shown below. To do this, we must first compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/d9/d99cb9753d63d0812245159b6727e30408eb6cd89d01ecb2f4090f65925527f1.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, $y$ approaches $0$. However, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, $y$ approaches $1$. Therefore, we have the following left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Left-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 0 \\\\ Right-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBoth of these limits exist. However, they are *not* the same. Consequently, the overall limit at $x=2$ does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cb3c51e-4afc-5b0f-9559-908488362a25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id 